WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.412
The directory and CA classes.

2
00:00:02.412 --> 00:00:08.313
ESC5 is unprivileged write access on the
forest's Public Key Services objects.

3
00:00:08.313 --> 00:00:13.204
The NTAuth certificates object holds the
CAs trusted to issue logon and domain

4
00:00:13.204 --> 00:00:16.687
controller certificates,
and each enrollment services

5
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:19.874
object records what a CA can
issue and who can enroll.

6
00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:23.288
Write access to either is
control over authentication.

7
00:00:23.288 --> 00:00:29.030
Defender for Identity has no ESC5
assessment, so review the ACLs, compare the

8
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:34.863
NTAuth store with your approved CA list,
and audit changes through event 5136.

9
00:00:34.863 --> 00:00:38.425
ESC6 is the CA-wide flag
that lets any requester

10
00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:42.262
supply subject alternative
names on any template.

11
00:00:42.262 --> 00:00:46.632
Find out why it was turned on, since
some old workflows depended on it,

12
00:00:46.632 --> 00:00:49.050
then clear it and restart the service.

13
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:54.351
The book corrects a common belief here:
KB5014754 introduced strong mapping,

14
00:00:54.351 --> 00:00:59.651
which reduces what a forged name can do
against Kerberos, but it doesn't change

15
00:00:59.651 --> 00:01:04.538
what the CA will sign, and other relying
parties may still trust the name.

16
00:01:04.538 --> 00:01:06.275
Clear the flag regardless.

17
00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:11.762
ESC7 is unprivileged Manage CA or
Issue and Manage Certificates.

18
00:01:11.762 --> 00:01:16.244
Build separate groups for CA
administrators, certificate managers,

19
00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:19.625
enrollment agents, auditors
and backup custodians.

20
00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:21.340
Service accounts count too:

21
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.889
the Intune Certificate Connector and
lifecycle tooling both hold Issue

22
00:01:25.889 --> 00:01:30.363
and Manage Certificates, so each is a
certificate manager in all but name.

23
00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:34.788
ESC8 is an HTTP enrollment
endpoint that accepts

24
00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:38.674
NTLM without HTTPS or Extended Protection.

25
00:01:38.674 --> 00:01:43.717
Microsoft's advisory: Extended
Protection set to Required,

26
00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:48.450
Require SSL, restart IIS, and
restrict NTLM where you can.

27
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.770
Better still, remove Web Enrollment
wherever nothing depends on it,

28
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.840
and apply the same rules to NDES.
